/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Wildcard Matching
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Dynamic Programming
Difficulty : Hard

Problem:
Given a string s and a pattern p, determine whether the entire
string matches the pattern.

The pattern supports:
    '?' -> Matches exactly one character.
    '*' -> Matches any sequence of characters, including empty.

The complete string must match the complete pattern.

Approach:
Use Dynamic Programming with space optimization.

Let dp[i][j] represent whether the first i characters of s
match the first j characters of p.

For a normal character or '?':
    dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]

because both the current string character and pattern
character must be consumed.

For '*', there are two possibilities:

1. '*' matches zero characters:
       dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-1]

2. '*' matches the current string character:
       dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j]

Therefore:

    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-1] || dp[i-1][j]

Why does this work?

A '*' is flexible because it can represent an empty sequence
or one or more characters.

If '*' matches nothing, we move only in the pattern.

If '*' matches the current character, we move in the string
while keeping '*' available to match more characters.

Since each DP state only depends on the previous row and the
current row, we do not need the complete 2D DP table.

Algorithm:
1. Initialize the empty string and empty pattern state.
2. Mark prefixes of the pattern containing only '*' as matches.
3. Process the string character by character.
4. For matching characters or '?', use the diagonal state.
5. For '*', use the current-row or previous-row state.
6. Move the current row into the previous row.
7. Return the final matching state.

Time Complexity : O(n × m)

Reason:
Every character-pattern pair is processed once.

Space Complexity : O(m)

Reason:
Only two DP rows of size m + 1 are maintained.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
private:

    bool solve(string& s, string& p) {

        int n = s.length();
        int m = p.length();

        // Previous and current DP rows.
        vector<int> prev(m + 1, false);
        vector<int> curr(m + 1, false);

        // Empty string matches an empty pattern.
        prev[0] = true;

        // An empty string can only match a pattern
        // consisting entirely of '*'.
        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {

            if (p[j - 1] == '*') {
                prev[j] = prev[j - 1];
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {

            // Reset the current row before processing it.
            fill(curr.begin(), curr.end(), false);

            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {

                // Normal character or '?' matches one character.
                if (s[i - 1] == p[j - 1] || p[j - 1] == '?') {
                    curr[j] = prev[j - 1];
                }

                // '*' can match:
                // 1. Zero characters -> curr[j - 1]
                // 2. Current character -> prev[j]
                else if (p[j - 1] == '*') {
                    curr[j] = prev[j] || curr[j - 1];
                }

                // Otherwise, the characters do not match.
                else {
                    curr[j] = false;
                }
            }

            // Current row becomes the previous row.
            prev = curr;
        }

        return prev[m];
    }

public:

    bool isMatch(string s, string p) {
        return solve(s, p);
    }
};
